            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for chair, then the Mandarin for here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 手机",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "手机 — shǒujī — \"mobile phone\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "这是我的手机 zhè shì wǒ de shǒujī — This is my phone."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 机 (jī) — the tree piece on the left, the one at the top left of 想, and on the right a two-stroke piece read jǐ, as in the word for how many. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "手机 (shǒujī) is built from 手 'hand' and 机 (jī) 'machine'. The second is inherited from Middle Chinese kj+j (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which the dictionary explains as the catch that sets off a crossbow. From that catch to any machine is the usual explanation, but the step is not given there."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for everyday answers."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
